<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Normandy landings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as D-Day, Operation Neptune · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Normandy landings of 6 June 1944, known as D-Day, were the Allied amphibious and airborne invasion of German-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that opened Operation Overlord. American, British and Canadian forces landed on five beaches, code-named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword, after airborne drops behind them. It was the largest seaborne invasion in history and led to the liberation of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/normandy-landings.jpg" alt="Normandy landings" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chief Photographer's Mate (CPHoM) Robert F. Sargent · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in June 1941, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> pressed his new allies to open a second front in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Winston Churchill persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> to postpone the invasion, since even with American help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> lacked adequate forces. Planning began in 1943. In the months before the landings the Allies carried out Operation Bodyguard, a deception meant to mislead the Germans about the date and place. Its part Fortitude South used a fictitious First United States Army Group under George S. Patton to suggest that the main attack would fall at Calais. Erwin Rommel commanded the German forces and the work on the Atlantic Wall, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> the Allied forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The planners needed a full moon, a tide midway between low and high and a landing shortly before dawn, which fit only a few days a month. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> tentatively chose 5 June, but high winds and low cloud forced a delay of 24 hours. The invasion began shortly after midnight on 6 June with bombing and the landing of 24,000 American, British and Canadian airborne troops. Amphibious landings followed at about 06:30 along an 80-kilometre stretch of coast divided into five sectors: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword.</p><p>Strong winds blew the landing craft east of their targets, particularly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> and Omaha. At Omaha, the most heavily defended beach, 27 of 32 amphibious tanks sank, and casualties were highest. Men of the 2nd Ranger Battalion scaled 30-metre cliffs at Pointe du Hoc and disabled guns found some distance away. Several fortified towns at Gold, Juno and Sword were cleared house by house.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> established beachheads at all five sites on the first day, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, a major objective, was not captured until 21 July. All five beachheads were not joined until 12 June. Allied casualties on the day were at least 10,000, with 4,414 confirmed dead, and German casualties are estimated at 4,000 to 9,000. Nearly 160,000 troops crossed the Channel on D-Day, and 875,000 had landed by the end of June. The Germans never achieved Hitler's stated aim of throwing the Allies back into the sea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Normandy landings” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…from Britain and went on to enlist more than a million men and women; its army fought in Italy and landed at Juno Beach on <b>D-Day</b>, its navy fought throughout the Battle of the Atlantic, and the British Commonwealth Air Training Plan trained over 130,000 aircrew in Canada.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet Operation Bagration in June 1944 destroyed Army Group Centre in the same month as the <b>Normandy landings</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>British and Commonwealth forces won at El Alamein in 1942, fought in Italy and Burma, and landed in Normandy on <b>D-Day</b>, 6 June 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…wrote the English words &quot;Lily of the Lamplight&quot; in 1944, Allied soldiers in Italy turned the tune into &quot;The <b>D-Day</b> Dodgers&quot;, and the Soviet officer Lev Kopelev wrote a German parody, now lost, to discourage enemy troops; the poet Joseph Brodsky later based a Russian poem on it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lili-marleen">Lili Marleen</a></li></ul></section>
